// The behavioural proof that the Bit COLLECTOR half of the safepoint handshake
// (#1633, runtime/stw/stw.bit) actually collects, that its root enumeration is
// complete, and that its sweep does not eat live objects.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ATTRIBUTION — WHY A COLLECTION COUNT HERE IS *THIS* COLLECTOR'S
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Pre-G2 (#1583) the deleted native runtime's `root` module was still linked
// and still owned the ABI name `bit_rt_safepoint`, so the poll codegen
// synthesized at every loop back edge went to that deleted runtime. Any
// `BIT_GC=stress BIT_GC_STATS=1` count read out of a normally linked binary
// was therefore that deleted runtime's collector's and was identical with or
// without this change — the trap #1632's agent measured (`collection 20000`)
// and correctly refused to claim.
//
// This test dodges it on both sides at once:
//
//   - The CODE under test is `stwPoll`, called by name. That is the Bit poll
//     body itself (`runtime/stw/stw.bit`), the function the `bit_rt_safepoint`
//     shim hands its snapshot to; nothing in the deleted native runtime's
//     `root` module can reach it.
//   - The HEAP under test is the `g` block below, a collector instance this
//     program builds over its own arena. The deleted native runtime's `root`
//     module's collector has never heard of it. Every number printed comes from `g`, so a non-zero
//     `gcStatCollections(g)` cannot have been produced by anything else.
//
// WHAT THIS CANNOT DRIVE, AND WHY (#1636). The natural end-to-end form would
// call the `@naked` shim `bit_rt_root_safepoint` so the snapshot is a real
// register/frame capture. It is not callable from Bit today: the INLINER inlines
// a `@naked` body into its caller, so the call site gets the shim's raw `ret`
// instead of a call and the caller returns with its frame never restored —
// filed as #1636 with a standalone repro. So the snapshot is supplied here
// instead, in exactly the `SafepointFrame` layout the shim would have built.
// The shim's own hand-encoded payload therefore remains UNEXECUTED by any test;
// that is #1636's to close, not this file's.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT EACH PHASE PROVES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   A — a poll COLLECTS, and every root class survives it. One payload is
//       reachable only as a buffered `is_ref` channel element (ABI.md §11), one
//       only as a registered task's `arg` (§5/§9), one only as a word on another
//       registered task's stack (§5). A fourth is reachable from nothing and
//       must be swept — without it the phase would pass on a collector that
//       marks everything.
//   B — remove all three roots, poll again, and all three are swept. This is
//       what proves the survivals in A were caused by the enumeration rather
//       than by a collection that never ran or never swept.
//   C — the PRECISE stack-map walk (`gcScanSnapshot`, §4) marks the slots a real
//       emitted stack map names. The table is decoded here independently of the
//       runtime's decoder, a frame is synthesized at a real
//       (code_addr + ret_off) safepoint, and the payload is planted in the first
//       slot that safepoint names. Marked -> survives; a second payload planted
//       nowhere is swept. Driven through the whole poll, not through
//       `gcScanSnapshot` alone, so the precise walk is proved to be WIRED INTO
//       the collection rather than merely to work in isolation.
//   D — the per-task SCRATCH area (#1679, #1683), the class that had no case
//       here and therefore no gate at all.
//   E — another stopped OS thread's PARKED FRAME (#1683), likewise.
//   F — the `bad=` counter the `BIT_GC_STATS` line grew in #1682, read through
//       the same bound-block accessor that line uses.
//   H — the RUNNING task's own stack (#1832). Unlike every phase above it does
//       NOT fabricate a stack: class 8 fires only for the task holding the real
//       `sp`, so the phase copies the real stack's bounds off the live current
//       task and lets the compiler place sixteen raw-`int` payloads, which
//       cannot all fit in the callee-saved file.
//   G — a parked task's own saved CALLEE-SAVED REGISTER FILE (#1742): the
//       `t[0..ctxWords)` block `schedSwitch` (`../../../runtime/sched`) writes
//       on every park. The task below has an EMPTY class-4 stack range (`sp`
//       set to its stack's own top) and a zero `arg`, so the only enumerated
//       class that can reach its payload is the ctx scan.
//   I — another stopped mutator's OWN STACK, conservatively (#1834). Phase E
//       proves that mutator's frame is walked PRECISELY; this proves the stack
//       BELOW that task's `ctxSp` and at or above the published frame is walked
//       CONSERVATIVELY, which is what a raw `int` needs and what a stack map
//       cannot give. Same synthetic-slot technique as E, with the payload
//       placed where every other class is empty by construction.
//   J — a thread parked in a `syscall` region (#1904, #2387): the class with
//       no case here and, until this change, no entry in `stw.bit`'s header
//       either — implemented and wired into `stwScanTasks` but enumerated
//       nowhere, so the declared/covered comparison below agreed with itself
//       while missing it entirely. Same `worldClaim` technique as E and I, but
//       published as `mSyscall` with a stack pointer (`stwSyscallLo`'s half)
//       rather than a frame, plus a second payload planted in the register
//       block `worldSyscallRegs` returns (`stwScanSyscallRegs`'s half, #1742's
//       gap over this state).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE CASE PER ROOT CLASS — THE RULE, AND WHY IT IS ENFORCED STATICALLY (#1683)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1679 was a whole root class covered by NOTHING: the per-task scratch area
// held two live references (`scrElem`, a blocking channel send/receive's value
// slot, and `scrErr`, the pending-error object) and no enumerated class reached
// them. Everything stayed green — `./make`, `libbitrt`, `rootpins`,
// `rootabi`, `stwwiring`, `./make test`, `test-stress` and the full
// differential sweep — because a missing root class is not a missing symbol and
// not a wrong dump. It is a live object swept, which shows up later, somewhere
// else, as a SIGSEGV on one platform and a wrong answer with exit 0 on another.
//
// This file already had the shape of the answer and only covered the classes
// its author had thought of, which is precisely the failure mode: a class
// nobody wrote is a class nobody tests. So the rule is now mechanical rather
// than aspirational — `runtime/stw/stw.bit`'s ROOT SET header enumerates the
// classes, every one of them carries a `ROOT CLASS <n>` marker here, and
// the deleted harness `stwwiring`'s "every enumerated root class has a case" test fails
// if the two sets ever differ or if the count falls below the floor. Adding a
// class to the collector without a case here is a RED BUILD, not a silent hole.
//
// Every phase asserts a NON-ZERO collection count of its own (`ran=`), because
// a straight-line Bit program collects zero times under `BIT_GC=stress` — the
// backends emit safepoints only at loop back edges — and a survival check
// across a collection that never happened proves nothing at all.

import { spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"
import { heapInit, heapWords } from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import { gcInit, gcConfigure, gcAlloc, gcOwns, gcNumObjects, gcStatCollections } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { smFormatVersion } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { gcWords, gcBindMemory, storeWord, loadWord, loadByte, reserveWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { worldWords, worldClaim, worldRelease, maxMutators, mParked, muWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { mSyscall, worldSyscallRegs } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { rtChanMake, chanBootBind, chanRegWords } from "../../../runtime/chan"
import { stwPollOn, stwAbandonedCount, stwBadOffsetsOn } from "../../../runtime/stw"
import { schedWords, scRegHead, taskWords, taskArg, taskState, taskParked } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { schedCurrentTask, taskAt } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { taskStackBase, taskStackBytes, ctxSp, taskRegNext } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { chanScratchWords, scrElem, scrErr, scrIfaceOk } from "../../../runtime/sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT `main` HOLDS, AND WHY EVERY BLOCK IS DECLARED THERE (#4249)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every backing `[]i64`/`[]i32` this program uses is allocated by `main` and
// stays live until `main`'s last line — the `held` prints at the end are what
// enforce it — so that only raw untraced pointers ever reach the collector
// under test and the LIVE (libbitrt) collector cannot move the ground under it
// when the suite reruns under `BIT_GC=stress`. That is the property under test,
// so the declarations below never move: the phase BODIES moved out
// (stwcollectphases.bit) and take what they plant into as parameters, or read
// its address out of the `wire` block, which holds addresses only.
//
// The blocks, in declaration order, and what each is for:
//
//   arena, indexArena, heapBlock, heapLock, listLock, state — the collector
//     under test and the heap it allocates from. `heapLock` and `listLock` are
//     distinct words on purpose (#3177): see `gcInit`'s own header
//     (runtime/gc/gc.bit) for why aliasing them now self-deadlocks.
//   meta — the TypeInfo table `buildMeta` fills: one leaf `Node`, the
//     `ref_array_info` sentinel, and phase F's malformed one.
//   worldBlock, schedBlock, chanReserve, reserveDesc, regBlock — the blocks
//     `boot` normally owns: the World/Mutator registry, a scheduler, and the
//     channel reserve + registry.
//   fakeTask, fakeStack — phase A's class-5 task. `fakeStack` stays 64 words on
//     purpose: it is the block whose short mapping caught the unbounded
//     265-word scratch read (#1679, back when 265 was the whole area). Phase H
//     reuses it as a stack range that provably does NOT hold the running `sp`.
//   longTask, longStack — phase A's class-4 task, on a stack longer than the
//     scratch area; see LONG_STACK_WORDS for why that placement is the point.
//   frameBuf, snapBuf — phase C's synthesized frame and this thread's published
//     `SafepointFrame`.
//   ctxTask, ctxStack — phase G's task and its deliberately unused stack.
//   cls8Task — phase H's task. It has NO stack array of its own, and that is
//     the whole point — see `phaseH`.
//   parkStackTask, parkedStack — phase I's task and the stack a synthesized
//     parked mutator claims to be standing in; see PARK_STACK_WORDS for why the
//     geometry is what it is.
//   syscallTask, syscallStack — phase J's, likewise, per SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS.
//   scratchTask, scratchStack — phase D's task, whose stack is exactly
//     `chanScratchWords` words, so the whole scratch area is mapped and the
//     scan's `min(chanScratchWords, stackBytes/8)` bound resolves to the full
//     area — that is what lets the payload at the LAST scratch word
//     (`scrIfaceOk`, 266 — #3280 moved this off `scrFsReadFailed`, 265, once
//     `chanScratchWords` widened past it) be a floor on the scanned set rather
//     than a second copy of the first word's test.
//   parkFrame, parkSnap — phase E's parked-thread frame and the
//     `SafepointFrame` that publishes it, both kept apart from phase C's so
//     that phase C's snapshot is not what phase E ends up proving.
//   wire — the addresses that are fixed once the collector is built, handed to
//     every phase. Addresses only; see stwcollectphases.bit.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PHASE CALLS, AND THE ROOT CLASS EACH ONE COVERS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every `ROOT CLASS <n>` marker in this program lives HERE, in this file and
// nowhere else: _tests_/bit/stwwiring reads `stwcollect.bit` by name and scans
// it for the markers, so a marker that followed its body into
// stwcollectphases.bit would silently drop out of coverage.
//
//   phaseAB — ROOT CLASS 1 (a buffered `is_ref` channel's element), ROOT CLASS 4
//             (a registered task's stack) and ROOT CLASS 5 (that task's `arg`),
//             then the same three roots removed and swept.
//   phaseC  — ROOT CLASS 2, this thread's own published snapshot, walked
//             precisely against a real emitted stack map.
//   phaseD  — ROOT CLASS 6, the per-task scratch area (#1679).
//   phaseE  — ROOT CLASS 3, another stopped OS thread's parked frame (#1683).
//   phaseF  — the `bad=` counter (#1682); no root class of its own.
//   phaseG  — ROOT CLASS 7, a parked task's saved callee-saved file (#1742).
//   phaseH  — ROOT CLASS 8, the RUNNING task's own stack (#1832).
//   phaseI  — ROOT CLASS 10, another stopped mutator's own stack (#1834).
//   phaseJ  — ROOT CLASS 9, a thread parked in a `syscall` region (#1904).
fn main() {
  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let indexArena = []i64(INDEX_WORDS)
  let heapBlock = []i64(heapWords)
  let heapLock = []i32(1)
  let listLock = []i32(1)
  let state = []i64(gcWords)
  let meta = []i64(META_WORDS)
  let worldBlock = []i64(worldWords)
  let schedBlock = []i64(schedWords)
  let chanReserve = []i64(CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS)
  let reserveDesc = []i64(reserveWords)
  let regBlock = []i64(chanRegWords)
  let fakeTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let fakeStack = []i64(TASK_STACK_WORDS)
  let longTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let longStack = []i64(LONG_STACK_WORDS)
  let frameBuf = []i64(FRAME_WORDS)
  let snapBuf = []i64(SNAP_WORDS)
  let ctxTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let ctxStack = []i64(TASK_STACK_WORDS)
  let cls8Task = []i64(taskWords)
  let parkStackTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let parkedStack = []i64(PARK_STACK_WORDS)
  let syscallTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let syscallStack = []i64(SYSCALL_STACK_WORDS)
  let scratchTask = []i64(taskWords)
  let scratchStack = []i64(chanScratchWords)
  let parkFrame = []i64(FRAME_WORDS)
  let parkSnap = []i64(SNAP_WORDS)
  let wire = []i64(WIRE_WORDS)

  buildMeta(meta, wire)
  wire[W_SCHED] = int(ptrOf(schedBlock))
  wire[W_REG] = int(ptrOf(regBlock))
  wire[W_SNAP] = int(ptrOf(snapBuf))
  wire[W_WORLD] = int(ptrOf(worldBlock))
  wire[W_FAKESTACK] = int(ptrOf(fakeStack))
  wire[W_ARENA] = int(ptrOf(arena))
  wire[W_INDEX] = int(ptrOf(indexArena))

  let g = ptrOf(state)
  if (!bootHeap(g, wire, heapBlock, heapLock, listLock)) {
    print("gcInit failed\n")
    return
  }
  // Wire the registry the mutator half uses and the three blocks the collector
  // half enumerates roots from. `stwBind` is all-or-nothing by design: with any
  // one of them 0 the poll refuses to collect rather than collecting with a
  // smaller root set.
  chanBootBind(
    int(ptrOf(reserveDesc)), int(ptrOf(chanReserve)), CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS * 8,
    int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)),
  )

  phaseAB(g, wire, fakeTask, longTask, longStack)
  phaseC(g, wire, frameBuf)
  phaseD(g, wire, worldBlock, scratchStack, scratchTask)
  phaseE(g, wire, worldBlock, parkFrame, parkSnap)
  phaseF(g, wire, scratchStack)
  phaseG(g, wire, ctxTask, ctxStack)
  phaseH(g, wire, cls8Task)
  phaseI(g, wire, worldBlock, parkStackTask, parkedStack)
  phaseJ(g, wire, worldBlock, syscallStack, syscallTask)

  // Seven groups, in declaration order, and the grouping carries no
  // meaning beyond fitting one line each: what matters is that every block
  // above is named by one of them, so none of them dies before this point.
  print("held ${len(arena) + len(indexArena) + len(heapBlock) + len(heapLock) + len(listLock)}\n")
  print("held ${len(state) + len(meta) + len(worldBlock) + len(schedBlock) + len(chanReserve)}\n")
  print("held ${len(reserveDesc) + len(regBlock) + len(fakeTask) + len(fakeStack) + len(longTask)}\n")
  print("held ${len(longStack) + len(frameBuf) + len(snapBuf) + len(ctxTask) + len(ctxStack)}\n")
  print("held ${len(cls8Task) + len(parkStackTask) + len(parkedStack) + len(syscallTask)}\n")
  print("held ${len(syscallStack) + len(scratchTask) + len(scratchStack) + len(parkFrame)}\n")
  print("held ${len(parkSnap) + len(wire)}\n")
}
